<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Zhongjia</h1><p class="st-meta">197 CE – 199 CE · capital Shouchun</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short-lived empire of the warlord Yuan Shu, who proclaimed himself emperor in 197 and so became the most despised of the Han warlords.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shu">Yuan Shu</a>, of the powerful Yuan family, controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a> region in the chaos after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a>. Possessing the imperial seal, taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-jian">Sun Jian's</a> family, and citing prophecies, he proclaimed himself emperor of Zhongjia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shouchun">Shouchun</a> in 197, while the Han emperor still lived.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime was notorious for extravagance: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shu">Yuan Shu's</a> palace was said to keep hundreds of concubines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> while famine reduced his soldiers to eating shellfish. His usurpation lost him allies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-ce">Sun Ce</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-bu">Lü Bu</a> and others turned against him.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-bu">Lü Bu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, his territory collapsing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shu">Yuan Shu</a> set out in 199 to join his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shao">Yuan Shao</a> in the north, was blocked and died, reportedly asking in vain for honey water. His claim illustrated how far Han authority had decayed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 197 CE – 199 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:63.62%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>197 CE – 199 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Shouchun</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yuan Shu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zhongjia (Yuan Shu)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=198&amp;focus=zhongjia" aria-label="Zhongjia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7677 994 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zhongjia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7997 1250L7769 1191"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7997 1250h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7997 1250h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Shouchun. <a href="/atlas?year=198&amp;focus=zhongjia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

